{0 Diagnostics}

Use diagnostics when you want to keep parsing and report multiple validation
problems instead of stopping at the first one.

{1 Types}

{@ocaml skip[
module Diagnostics : sig
  type 'd entry = { pos : int; value : 'd }
  type 'd t
  val create : unit -> 'd t
  val to_list : 'd t -> 'd entry list
  val length : 'd t -> int
  val is_empty : 'd t -> bool
  val clear : 'd t -> unit
end
]}

A {!type:Parseff.Diagnostics.t} collects the diagnostics of one parse. Its type
parameter is the type of the values given to {!val:Parseff.warn}, and
{!val:Parseff.Diagnostics.to_list} returns the same type. The two cannot
differ.

{1 Emitting diagnostics}

{@ocaml skip[val warn : 'd Diagnostics.t -> 'd -> unit]}

{!val:Parseff.warn} records a non-fatal diagnostic at the current parser
position.

{@ocaml skip[val warn_at : 'd Diagnostics.t -> pos:int -> 'd -> unit]}

{!val:Parseff.warn_at} records a non-fatal diagnostic at an explicit position.

{1 Collecting diagnostics}

Create a collector and pass it to the runner with [~diagnostics]. The runner
empties it first. The parser must warn into the same collector. [warn] into a
collector that the runner did not get does nothing.

The result of the parse is the usual {!type:Parseff.error} result. Read the
diagnostics from the collector after the parse. They are there for [Ok] and for
[Error].

{@ocaml skip[val parse :
  ?max_depth:int ->
  ?consume:[ `All | `Partial ] ->
  ?diagnostics:'d Diagnostics.t ->
  string ->
  (unit -> 'a) ->
  ('a, error) Stdlib.result]}

{@ocaml skip[Parseff.parse input parser]}

By default the parse requires that all input is consumed. It is the same as
calling {!val:Parseff.end_of_input} once more at the end of your parser. It does
not change how explicit {!val:Parseff.end_of_input} calls behave inside your
parser. Pass [~consume:`Partial] to let the parse stop before the end of the
input.
{!val:Parseff.parse_source} takes the same arguments.

{1 Example}

{@ocaml[
type validation = [ `Octet_out_of_range of int ]

let number_lenient diagnostics () =
  let start = Parseff.position () in
  let digits = Parseff.many ~at_least:1 Parseff.digit () in
  let n = List.fold_left (fun acc d -> (acc * 10) + d) 0 digits in
  if n > 255 then
    Parseff.warn_at diagnostics ~pos:start (`Octet_out_of_range n);
  n

let ip_address_lenient diagnostics () =
  let a = number_lenient diagnostics () in
  let _ = Parseff.char '.' in
  let b = number_lenient diagnostics () in
  let _ = Parseff.char '.' in
  let c = number_lenient diagnostics () in
  let _ = Parseff.char '.' in
  let d = number_lenient diagnostics () in
  (a, b, c, d)

let () =
  let diagnostics : validation Parseff.Diagnostics.t =
    Parseff.Diagnostics.create ()
  in
  let outcome =
    Parseff.parse ~diagnostics "999.2.3.256"
      (ip_address_lenient diagnostics)
  in
  (match outcome with
  | Ok (a, b, c, d) -> Printf.printf "Parsed: %d.%d.%d.%d\n" a b c d
  | Error error ->
      Printf.printf "Parse error at %d: %s\n" error.range.start
        (Parseff.error_message error));
  List.iter
    (fun ({ pos; value } : validation Parseff.Diagnostics.entry) ->
      match value with
      | `Octet_out_of_range n ->
          Printf.printf "  at %d: octet %d out of range (0-255)\n" pos n)
    (Parseff.Diagnostics.to_list diagnostics)
]}

{1 Backtracking semantics}

Diagnostics are transactional with parser control flow:

- {!val:Parseff.or_}: diagnostics from failed branches are rolled back
- {!val:Parseff.many}: diagnostics from the final failing attempt are rolled
  back
- {!val:Parseff.look_ahead}: diagnostics are rolled back

This prevents diagnostics from leaking out of speculative branches.

{1 State that rolls back}

A [ref] that a parser changes keeps its new value when a branch backtracks.
Use a {!type:Parseff.State.t} cell instead when the value must follow the
branch, as diagnostics do: a symbol table, an indentation stack, a counter.

{@ocaml skip[
module State : sig
  type 'a t
  val create : 'a -> 'a t
  val get : 'a t -> 'a
  val set : 'a t -> 'a -> unit
end
]}

A {!val:Parseff.State.set} in a branch that backtracks is undone with the
branch: the failed branch of {!val:Parseff.or_} and {!val:Parseff.one_of}, a
failed {!val:Parseff.optional}, the last iteration of {!val:Parseff.many},
{!val:Parseff.sep_by} and {!val:Parseff.fold_left}, and
{!val:Parseff.look_ahead}, also when it succeeds. After {!val:Parseff.commit}
or {!val:Parseff.cut} seals a branch, a failure no longer undoes the sets in it.

{@ocaml[
let depth = Parseff.State.create 0

let open_paren () =
  let _ = Parseff.char '(' in
  Parseff.State.set depth (Parseff.State.get depth + 1)

let () =
  match
    Parseff.parse "x"
      (Parseff.or_
         (fun () ->
           open_paren ();
           Parseff.char ')')
         (fun () -> Parseff.char 'x'))
  with
  | Ok _ -> Printf.printf "depth = %d\n" (Parseff.State.get depth)
  | Error _ -> ()
]}

This prints [depth = 0]: the left branch failed before it set the cell. With
input [(y], the left branch sets [depth] to [1], fails on [y], and the set is
undone before the right branch runs.

An exception that a branch does not catch skips the undo. A
{!val:Parseff.fail}, an {!val:Parseff.error} or an exception of your own raised
inside {!val:Parseff.or_}, {!val:Parseff.optional}, {!val:Parseff.many},
{!val:Parseff.sep_by} or {!val:Parseff.fold_left} leaves the cell with the value
it had when the exception was raised. {!val:Parseff.look_ahead} restores it.
Diagnostics behave the same way.

Only the value in the cell is restored. A mutable value inside the cell, such
as a [Hashtbl.t], keeps its changes, so store immutable values. The runner does
not take the cell, and the value at the end of a parse stays in it. A
{!val:Parseff.State.set} raises [Invalid_argument] when another running parse
set the same cell.
